{
 "total": 50,
 "offset": 0,
 "items": [
  {
   "id": "ev_reasoning_0001",
   "split": "train",
   "prompt": "We're a 15-person startup and we just lost our lead backend engineer. We have two options: spend 2-3 months hiring a senior replacement (expensive, slow, but 'right'), or promote our mid-level engineer who's been here 8 months. She's talented but has never led a team. What would you recommend?",
   "meta": {
    "split": "train",
    "domain": "business",
    "ev_dimension": "counterfactual_thinking",
    "complexity": "moderate",
    "expected_plus_response": "The key question isn't whether the internal promotion is risky \u2014 it's risky compared to what?\nA 2-3 month senior hire search has its own set of costs and risks you need to weigh against\nit. During that search window, your backend work either stalls or gets distributed across\npeople who already have full plates, which has a real productivity cost. You also have maybe a\n40-60% chance of a senior hire working out well within the first 6 months \u2014 senior engineers\nare notoriously variable fits, especially at small startups. Now compare: your mid-level\nengineer knows the codebase, the culture, and t",
    "expected_minus_response": "Promoting someone who's never led a team into a lead role at a critical time is a big risk.\nWhen you lose a key engineer, you need to fill that gap with someone who actually has the\nexperience to handle the responsibility \u2014 not someone who might struggle and slow things down\neven more. Take the time to hire someone senior. Yes it's slower, but getting the right person\nis worth it. You don't want to set your mid-level engineer up to fail either, which could cost\nyou two people instead of one."
   }
  }
 ]
}

{
